00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:24.470
So I will give first
a design overview.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:30.760
This is our platform
in very high level.

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:34.950
We collect events
from the clients

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.799
and we produce those
events into Pub/Sub.

00:08:38.799 --> 00:08:42.159
This help us to collect
data from all our data

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:43.090
centers we use.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:48.780
We still have own brand data
centers and also Google ones.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.850
But anyhow, we collect all
this data around the world.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:54.280
We use the amazing
Google network,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:58.360
and then we pull the
data from our consumers

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:01.120
and then make it
available in Google Cloud

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.450
Storage as a set of files.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:11.850
We have a specific format that
we will talk about this later.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:16.800
And yes, because we
want this event delivery

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.600
system to be a self service, we
have an automation deployment

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.260
that our developers just
configure what events they want

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.190
to send and we are able to
deploy the processor using

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:35.190
Google Cloud API, so we don't
have to be blocking anybody.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.800
They just configure and
are able to deliver.

00:09:41.274 --> 00:09:42.440
GIOVANNI GALLORO: Thank you.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:49.980
In the design, Nelson showed
that there are three hexagons,

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:53.080
three Google Cloud
Platform components.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:55.100
One of these is Pub/Sub.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:58.590
Pub/Sub is a messaging
service that--

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.560
completely manage
a messaging service

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.290
that provide
asynchronous messaging

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:07.020
between independent
applications.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.410
It basically implements
publisher subscriber

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:16.230
architecture where there
is a publishing application

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:19.350
submitting a
message into a topic

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.020
and a subscribing
application that

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.440
has subscribed to
a topic of interest

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:32.280
and retrieved the message
when it comes online.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.390
Pub/Sub can act as a
buffer between application

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:39.360
because its message store
can hold the messages

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:44.430
until the subscribing
application is ready to receive

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:48.560
the message, and it has
a dedicated capacity

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:51.000
in all Google Cloud
Platform region.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:55.380
It has been tested to
have a subsecond timing

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:59.430
in notification and in
message delivery, even when it

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:04.260
has been tested at one
million events per second.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:09.840
And it use HTTP, so the
publishing application

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.280
could be any application
supporting HTTP GET request.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:17.820
Subscribing application,
connecting two ways,

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:21.930
can in a pull way,
it has to support

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:27.660
HTTP GET requests, and in a
push mode where Pub/Sub push

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:30.470
messages directly
to the application,

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:35.640
the subscribing application,
as to expose [? WEbook ?]

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:39.060
supporting HTTP post request.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.670
Another component is
Google Cloud Storage.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:47.190
It's not just storage
where data stored

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:49.580
and a server does binary object.

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:53.790
It supports an unlimited
number of binary objects

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:57.210
with a maximum size
of five terabyte.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:12:02.730
And it can be used, for
example, to serve web content,

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:08.160
because it's accessible
with HTTP protocol,

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.780
with standard protocol.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:15.900
It can be used to store data
related to archival or disaster

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.870
recovery or to provide
to users access

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:22.410
to download access to big files.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:26.730
Google uses this service,
this Google Cloud Storage,

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:30.840
as a back end storage for media
files, [INAUDIBLE] photos,

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:34.890
for attachments in Gmail, and
for all the other documents

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.520
in Drive.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:40.230
It provide replication
and high availability,

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:44.560
so customer doesn't have to
manage high availability.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:51.950
It is accessible, as I say, with
HTTP, with XML, API, JSON API,

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:56.040
and with the Google Cloud
Platform console itself,

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.190
or js.util command line.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.010
It provides different
storage classes.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:06.476
This difference is
SLA, multi-regional,

00:13:06.476 --> 00:13:12.640
and regional are for data
accessed very frequently.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:17.850
Multi-regional, you have data
replicated between two regions

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:20.640
in the same macro area--

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:23.810
for example, Europe,
in US, or in Asia.

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:27.450
In regional, you have
data replicated only

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:28.750
inside one region.

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:31.540
And Nearline is for
data that is accessed

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:34.740
less than one time a
month, and Codeline

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:39.210
is for data archival
storage, mainly,

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:43.740
for data that is accessed
less than once a year.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.590
Then we have BigQuery.

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:49.350
BigQuery is basically
completely managed service

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:53.910
that provides the
users the ability

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:58.800
to perform queries,
analytics, reports

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:05.610
on huge data set with the
very first performance.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:07.470
Our equivalent of
an enterprise data

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.180
warehouse with the
difference that users

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:14.130
are only to log data
and perform the queries.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:21.060
Users doesn't have to allocate
capacity, provision server.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:25.050
User doesn't have the
notion of capacity neither,

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:31.290
because capacity is allocated
dynamically on demand when user

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:35.250
perform the query based
on the amount of data

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:38.890
on which the query
are performed.

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:49.980
And it's support access via
JDBC, ODBC via BigQuery API,

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:54.960
command line, and an HTTP UI.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.515
And then back to the
Spotify architecture.

00:14:58.515 --> 00:15:00.340
NELSON ARAPE: Thank you.

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:05.170
So first diagram
was very high level.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:07.020
This is a level deeper.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:12.970
This is still the
three big area,

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:15.930
so we will start with
the producer side.

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:19.970
So as I told you before, we
produce more than 1.5 millions

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:22.740
of [INAUDIBLE] per second.

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:26.430
This is like average, because
there is a daily pattern

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:28.130
and we have high
tide and low tide.

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:28.630
So.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:33.560
And this is how it looks.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:37.120
We have our client-sent events.

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:40.040
They eventually will
reach our microservice

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:42.590
in our data centers.

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:49.380
So each of these microservice
will write the event as a log

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:51.570
line, as a cislog log line.

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.080
As text.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:59.020
So on those holes, we
have deployed the daemon.

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:01.750
We call it the File Tailer
because what it does

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:02.860
is just that.

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:05.730
It tails the log
line and make sure

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:10.930
that that event has
been pushed successfully

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.230
to our even-driven system.

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:15.090
It reads line by line.

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:18.840
It's very dumb, doesn't
have much logic.

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:23.260
Send it to our next
component, event service.

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:26.290
And then if you get
200, it says, yes, it

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:29.940
was persistent successfully.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:34.050
It also-- this log line
would be like the stats

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:37.330
so it will receive
an invalid code.

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:43.250
So, OK, I don't need to-- for
drive, but if it's not at 200,

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:45.730
this is not a invalid
code so he will

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:48.930
re-try to make sure the
data have been pushed

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:52.590
and we don't lose any data.

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:56.480
This is where the
events log is happening.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:00.470
Here, we parse the text line.

00:17:00.470 --> 00:17:02.750
We have a schema
definition, so we'll

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:08.550
make sure that these
match one of our events.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:11.810
We also converted
to Avro because we

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:16.550
will push Avro binary from
this point until then.

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:21.480
And we also check these
events against wireless,

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.680
because it's easier for us
to changing in the wireless

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:29.900
to prevent pushing some
event than rolling out

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:33.510
changes to our clients to
stop producing that event.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:37.370
So spending is good,
but waste is not.

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:40.470
This is our motto in Spotify.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:43.350
So if we don't need
to send that event,

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:47.860
we will just say, no,
invalid, don't re-try.

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:54.210
So if everything is OK,
then we push this event

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:57.870
to Google Pub/Sub.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.450
And we use one topic
per event type.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:03.150
So this provides very
good acceleration.

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:06.790
We have-- not all the
events are the same.

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:13.760
And we have metrics related
to the specific event type.

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:15.720
So we like it this way.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:24.300
Then, on the consumer side,
we have all those events

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:26.370
arriving in Pub/Sub topics.

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:28.960
And we create
sub-creations and then

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:33.190
we pull that data
using ETL processes.

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:37.090
I will explain further
how this ETL looks like.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:40.330
But then, once it
pulls that data,

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:43.780
we do some touching
to that data,

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:45.640
for example, removing duplicate.

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:49.210
Our clients resend
events, and also

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:53.090
being a distributed
architecture,

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:55.040
could have duplicates
all the way.

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:57.920
So this is a component
that handles that.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:01.000
And then makes it available
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:04.510
And we use a very
specific format.

00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:05.790
This is how it looks.

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:11.610
We use cloud storage hash,
something called Buckets,

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:14.860
let's say like a
drive, a place where

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:18.820
I can store objects or files.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:26.270
But inside this Bucket, we have
a semantic bucket of event--

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.760
sorry, date and time.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:33.010
So we say, OK, this specific
date, this specific hour, we

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:35.860
will store in that
folder all the data that

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:39.470
have been happening for
this specific event.

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:46.210
So once we think we
consumed all the data,

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:49.120
we say, OK, we mark
this out as complete.

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:52.060
So this is how it looks in time.

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:55.600
We are making this
data as complete

00:19:55.600 --> 00:20:00.820
because until that point is when
our downstream user will start

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:03.130
to run and consume this data.

00:20:03.130 --> 00:20:10.180
We don't want to say, OK,
down through independencies,

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:14.860
start your crunching time, and
then later modify that dataset,

00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:18.030
because then we will
need to re-trigger

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:21.010
our dependencies,
downstream dependencies

00:20:21.010 --> 00:20:25.710
And then they will need to read
through their dependencies too.

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:30.240
So we take care
of not doing that.

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:34.910
We treat-- once we mark
a folder as complete,

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:38.790
we don't allow to
modify that data.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:42.580
But because everything
could happen,

00:20:42.580 --> 00:20:46.390
let's say we have an hour that
we already marked as complete,

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:50.040
but suddenly late data
arrives that hour.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:52.590
This will happen
because we use heuristic

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:57.660
to determine when it's
time to close the hour.

00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:06.940
So the heuristic is not
always 100% fine every time.

00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:10.770
So what we do is that, yes,
this is already closed.

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:13.410
But then we will
move these few events

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:18.300
that arrived late to the
next hour that is still open.

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:21.720
So our downstream users
are aware of this.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:28.380
So they will know that if,
for example, this hour three

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:33.660
potentially could contain events
from hour two, for example.

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:34.160
So--

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:43.500
GIOVANNI GALLORO: So
in this consumer side,

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:47.260
Nelson will introduce
other components that are

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:48.910
part of Google Cloud Platform.

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:51.880
One of those components
is Cloud SQL.

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:55.740
Cloud SQL is basically
completely managing

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:59.200
the database of the
service offering

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:02.260
that provide the
application level access

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:07.270
to MySQL DB or Postgres DB.

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:10.920
Users have to configure only
the compute capacity needed,

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:14.450
the storage capacity needed,
if they want replication

00:22:14.450 --> 00:22:17.110
and the Google Cloud
Platform automation

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:20.140
manage operating system
patching backups,

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:23.110
and replication between zones.

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:26.290
It explores basic
connection stream

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:32.660
that you can use, the user
can use, in their application.

00:22:32.660 --> 00:22:35.210
Another component
of that is used

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:38.930
in the consumer part of the
Spotify event delivery system

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:40.210
Cloud DataProc.

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:46.250
Cloud DataProc is a
management service, again,

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:53.035
that provides Hadoop and Spark
jobs, Hadoop and Spark cluster.

00:22:53.035 --> 00:22:56.210
It automates the deployment
of the cluster, the management

00:22:56.210 --> 00:23:00.860
of the cluster, and Spark
and Hadoop jobs always

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:04.600
require a large amount of
data, a large number of nodes.

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:09.120
So in this case, the elasticity
and scalability of cloud

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:13.910
can happen because, for example,
you can pay the resource only

00:23:13.910 --> 00:23:15.930
when you have a job to run.

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:20.540
And then you can shut down the
nodes after the job has run.

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:26.300
You can have the resources
immediately available.

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:27.950
And You don't have
to wait for a server

00:23:27.950 --> 00:23:31.220
to be acquired, the cabling,
install it, then the software

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:33.110
to be configured.

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:37.460
And you can scale at the
maximum extent, number of nodes.

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:41.540
So the fact that you are
not allocating capacity

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.340
but you are using
capacity only when

00:23:43.340 --> 00:23:48.860
you need to run the job
enables you to run it

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:54.020
at the maximum number of nodes
and finish the job sooner

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:57.030
and having the business
results quicker.

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:04.639
And then let's go to the
rest of the architecture.

00:24:04.639 --> 00:24:05.430
NELSON ARAPE: Yeah.

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:05.929
OK.

00:24:05.929 --> 00:24:11.660
So this is assuming of
what our ETL looks like.

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:16.350
And we decide to build our
ETL as three microservices,

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:21.240
And Consumer,
Completionist and Deduper.

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:24.810
So the consumers is
a very dumb process.

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:30.210
He pulls data from
Pub/Sub, and then it

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:32.710
will write that data as--

00:24:32.710 --> 00:24:34.940
I mean, he pulls
a batch of events,

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:39.600
and will write this batch
as a small file in the one

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:41.820
intermediate Google
Cloud Storage.

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:44.130
So we put it there.

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:46.190
And then we notify
this other component,

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:47.920
called completionist.

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:49.890
I will explain
more what it does.

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:52.700
And when the completionist
say, OK, yes,

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:58.380
I persist your
notification that you

00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:01.230
have a new file with
data, then that's

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:05.820
when the consumer
acknowledge Pub/Sub.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:09.210
Yes, I have consumed
successfully this patch

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:10.530
of events.

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:13.740
If something happens in
these, and for some reason

00:25:13.740 --> 00:25:17.200
we are not able, or the
completionist didn't return

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:21.030
or we have some problem writing
to Google Cloud Storage,

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:24.960
we know that because we didn't
acknowledge those events,

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:29.610
those events are still
safe stored in the Pub/Sub.

00:25:29.610 --> 00:25:32.100
So by doing this,
we are making sure

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:35.170
that we are safely moving
the data from Pub/Sub

00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:38.170
and make it available
as files in Google

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:42.940
Cloud, this intermediate
bucket in Google Cloud Storage.

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:46.900
So this Completionist,
the name is

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:49.600
because this is the
component that decides

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:51.880
when an hour is complete.

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:58.730
So for doing that, we use
Cloud SQL as a database.

00:25:58.730 --> 00:26:01.140
So when you receive
a notification

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:05.380
that the consumer has used
this file for this event

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:08.230
at this date and
hour, so who will

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:09.910
store that in the database?

00:26:09.910 --> 00:26:14.860
And this is where the heuristic
of completing the hour

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:16.150
is stored.

00:26:16.150 --> 00:26:19.750
So here we use, when
was the last time

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:23.030
that consumer wrote a file?

00:26:23.030 --> 00:26:27.675
How the bubble in the
Google Pub/Sub looks like?

00:26:27.675 --> 00:26:30.280
We are able to get
a metric and say,

00:26:30.280 --> 00:26:33.430
where is the time stamp
of all these events that

00:26:33.430 --> 00:26:35.560
haven't been acknowledged yet?

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:40.280
So there, we know, yes, we
still have data on Google Cloud

00:26:40.280 --> 00:26:43.700
Storage, so we cannot close
this hour at this moment.

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:47.830
So once all these
conditions have met,

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:51.460
the completion is then
decided to close an hour.

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:53.904
And then, if the--

00:26:53.904 --> 00:26:55.320
for example, in
the producer side,

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:56.940
this is what could happen.

00:26:56.940 --> 00:27:02.800
Suddenly we omit events, a
few events, for all the hours.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:06.070
So consumers will put it,
and then the completion

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:08.690
will say, OK, no, sorry, we
are already closed this hour,

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:10.090
and we'll move it the next.

00:27:13.250 --> 00:27:16.130
Then, the last component,
the Deduper, the name

00:27:16.130 --> 00:27:20.300
is because here is when
we do the duplication.

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:26.550
This is a MapReduce job
that runs on DataProc.

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:29.650
We have a scheduled
service, it's open source,

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:35.460
called Sticks, that when it's
time to run, we say, please run

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.650
this, the duplication
job, every hour.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:42.410
So when it's time to run,
it will trigger the job.

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:45.070
Then Deduper first
asks the completion,

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:46.900
is this hour complete?

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:49.610
If it is, it will
return the list of files

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:51.510
from the intermediate storage.

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:54.980
So this is the list of files I
need to read and the duplicate.

00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:59.980
So after this
initialization, the Deduper

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:04.040
just has to produce the runs
and the duplication, which we--

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:07.630
this is written
in Apache Crunch.

00:28:07.630 --> 00:28:12.350
And we sprung a DataProc
cluster just to run that job.

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:18.260
And when it's finished, we turn
down that DataProc cluster.

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:22.910
And yes, we make it available
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:28:22.910 --> 00:28:28.070
So I have explained how we
produce, how we consume.

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:31.500
Now, the missing part is
how this gets configured.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:33.050
As I told you
before, we don't want

00:28:33.050 --> 00:28:38.040
to be a team that blocks
other developers in Spotify.

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:44.270
So we need to manage
these 250 event types.

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:46.470
And this presents
two challenges.

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:49.910
One is that, yes, this
is a lot of event types

00:28:49.910 --> 00:28:53.690
that we need to make it in a
way that it can be configurable.

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:57.170
And the other is that event
types are not the same.

00:28:57.170 --> 00:29:00.430
We have, for example,
one event type is--

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:03.920
every time a user
has played a song.

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:05.570
But we have other events like.

00:29:05.570 --> 00:29:07.640
For example, a feature
we are developing

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:12.300
that we-- only a few beta
testers are using that feature.

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:16.640
So we go from hundreds
of events per second

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:19.360
to only a few events per minute.

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:24.350
So we need a way to
scale the consumption

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:28.310
to know, yes, for this event
type we need to pull a lot.

00:29:28.310 --> 00:29:32.520
But for this other set,
with only a few per minute

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:35.190
this is enough.

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:41.920
GIOVANNI GALLORO: So to
address these scale needs,

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:44.850
what are the services
and the capabilities

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:47.700
that are in Google
Cloud Platform?

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:52.200
You can scale instances
in a vertical way,

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:56.290
so you can add more capacity
to a single instance,

00:29:56.290 --> 00:30:00.330
you can do that on Google Cloud
Platform with the advantage

00:30:00.330 --> 00:30:03.990
that you have a custom
VM types, so you

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:10.050
can provide to an instance
exactly the amount of CPU core

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:13.920
and the amount of RAM that
you need, no less, no more.

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:19.610
You don't have to be
bound to fix the VM side,

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:24.650
it's where there is a predefined
ratio between the number

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:26.910
of core and gigabytes of RAM.

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:29.250
And this is very helpful.

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:34.440
And during the usage, your
resource usage is monitored,

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:39.540
and you get from Google Cloud
Platform console suggestions.

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:42.600
For example, if you
are not currently

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:45.600
using efficiently
or your capacity,

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:48.390
you have a suggestion to
downsize your instance.

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:50.730
And you have estimate
of the building

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:53.580
saving that you can do.

00:30:53.580 --> 00:30:55.320
On the other side,
if you are reaching

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:58.050
the peak of the
capacity you provided,

00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:01.980
you have a suggestion to
increase the capacity assigned

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:03.690
to your VMs.

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:07.860
But vertical scalability is
often not enough, for example,

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:10.530
because you reach
a limit and you

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:15.150
are unable to scale
to provide, to serve

00:31:15.150 --> 00:31:18.780
all these number of users,
this number of events.

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:22.290
So if you want to
overcome this limit,

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:24.360
you have to use
horizontal scaling,

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:28.410
you have to add instances to
multiply the resources assigned

00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:30.390
to your service.

00:31:30.390 --> 00:31:32.070
You do this in
Google Cloud Platform

00:31:32.070 --> 00:31:37.710
with managed instance group,
where a set of instance

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.680
is managed as a single entity.

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:43.710
They are completely
equal among them,

00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:48.690
so their configuration is
based on an instance template

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:52.020
where you decide
where this base image

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:54.999
on which the instances
have to be based on,

00:31:54.999 --> 00:31:57.540
what is the configuration, what
is the network configuration,

00:31:57.540 --> 00:31:59.760
the startup script, et cetera.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:01.500
And then you deploy
these instances.

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:05.520
You can have regional
manager distance group, where

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:08.580
you deploy distances
across different zones

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:11.570
in the same region, and
you have a load balancer

00:32:11.570 --> 00:32:17.220
spread in traffic between them,
so you have full tolerance,

00:32:17.220 --> 00:32:18.550
you have high availability.

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:24.100
If one or more instance or
a complete zone goes down.

00:32:24.100 --> 00:32:25.830
And you have out of scale too.

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:31.980
So you can figure auto
scaling, so instances

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:35.580
can be added to your
manage instance group

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:39.960
if your load increases, and
can be removed if your load

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:40.620
decrease.

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:42.780
And you can do that based
on different metrics--

00:32:42.780 --> 00:32:47.790
for example, CPU usage,
or number of requests

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:54.980
hitting your service,
or a custom metric, too.

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:57.930
You can do running upgrade if
you need to upgrade your code.

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:01.260
You can update your
instance template

00:33:01.260 --> 00:33:04.710
and trigger a
controller update that

00:33:04.710 --> 00:33:08.430
will update your service
in a progressive way,

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:10.470
not disrupting your services.

00:33:10.470 --> 00:33:12.250
Your user continues to work.

00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:13.980
And you can configure
how to [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:18.600
so when your instance doesn't
respond to an alt-check

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:22.320
that you configure it, your
instance will be killed

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:26.010
and will be replaced.

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:26.870
NELSON ARAPE: OK.

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:34.090
So going back to deployment
automation, so we have our--

00:33:34.090 --> 00:33:38.210
which events are defined
in system, defined--

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:40.670
we have a distributed
configured service.

00:33:40.670 --> 00:33:42.420
And we use these--

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:46.350
first, of the event service
to populate the wireless,

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:51.840
so we are able to create those
[? R ?] nodes in this wireless.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:54.900
And we have a
deployment service that

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:57.170
talks to Google Cloud Engine.

00:33:57.170 --> 00:34:04.260
And then, every ETL
is a managed group,

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:11.219
DCE managed group with an
autoscalar policy of CPU usage.

00:34:11.219 --> 00:34:16.620
So our consumer, the
ones we treat this way,

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:21.480
it's very simple because we
don't need to handle how big

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:22.860
or how small is the band.

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:28.370
We just say, OK, we're
pulling on the more back log

00:34:28.370 --> 00:34:33.120
listening, Google in
Pub/Sub, the more CPU usage

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:36.840
will consume this consumer, and
then autoscale school policy

00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:39.060
will decide, oh,
looks like we need

00:34:39.060 --> 00:34:44.730
more VMs because the threshold
is higher than the one we set.

00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:48.600
So we have autoscaler
deploying more VMs.

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:54.060
So we are pulling more data from
Pub/Sub but across many VMs.

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:57.690
And we decide to use also
more VMs so we don't waste

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:01.500
too much resources on
those few events that are

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:04.870
only a few events per minute.

00:35:04.870 --> 00:35:09.120
So we have more VMs, but we
have with autoscaler, too.

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:12.090
For those we need
more resources,

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:15.000
please spend more
machines, or more VMs.

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:22.200
GIOVANNI GALLORO: OK.

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:23.380
I think we are done.

00:35:23.380 --> 00:35:25.910
I don't know if there is
time for questions, or--

00:35:31.550 --> 00:35:33.000
We have four minutes, right?

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:38.430
If there are questions.

00:35:38.430 --> 00:35:41.150
NELSON ARAPE: Anyways,
we will be outside here,

00:35:41.150 --> 00:35:43.444
if you want to
approach personally.

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:49.930
No No questions, probably.

00:35:49.930 --> 00:35:50.560
Oh, a question.

00:35:56.780 --> 00:35:57.860
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:35:57.860 --> 00:36:00.260
Why are you
processing the message

00:36:00.260 --> 00:36:05.690
before pushing them to
Pub/Sub, and why you're not

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:08.870
processing them after--

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:09.650
NELSON ARAPE: So--

00:36:09.650 --> 00:36:12.604
AUDIENCE: --after trailing
them from Pub/Sub.

00:36:12.604 --> 00:36:14.020
NELSON ARAPE: You
say why we don't

00:36:14.020 --> 00:36:15.670
push from clients to Pub/Sub?

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:16.470
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:19.700
Something, or-- I mean, down
the interface between the two.

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:21.770
Because here you're doing some--

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:25.920
applying some logic that
could be time-consuming.

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:27.620
NELSON ARAPE: Yes.

00:36:27.620 --> 00:36:31.340
There are two answers for that.

00:36:31.340 --> 00:36:33.380
One is legacy.

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:36.740
The straight answer
is that before, we

00:36:36.740 --> 00:36:39.630
used a system based
on Kafka, and I

00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:46.100
can talk about some scars that
I have because of that system.

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:49.730
So we decided to
build the system,

00:36:49.730 --> 00:36:53.630
keeping the same API in the
beginning and at the end.

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:55.430
Right now, we are
working on a library

00:36:55.430 --> 00:36:59.390
to push, to public
directory from here to--

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:02.540
sorry, from here, we still
want to-- those services,

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:04.520
we want still some control.

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:07.230
We don't want to
push credentials

00:37:07.230 --> 00:37:10.400
to all our microservices.

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:14.249
But we will keep these two
steps and publish directly.

00:37:14.249 --> 00:37:15.227
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:37:15.227 --> 00:37:16.205
Thank you.

00:37:24.344 --> 00:37:25.510
GIOVANNI GALLORO: Thank you.

00:37:25.510 --> 00:37:26.240
We are done.

00:37:26.240 --> 00:37:28.020
Thank you.

